--- !u!114 &5549199957838310464
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 6933968242922362548}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: f4688fdb7df04437aeb418b961361dc5, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  m_Material: {fileID: 0}
  m_Color: {r: 1, g: 1, b: 1, a: 1}
  m_RaycastTarget: 1
  m_RaycastPadding: {x: 0, y: 0, z: 0, w: 0}
  m_Maskable: 1
  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_text: "Name: Mackenzie Joyce\r\nAge: 21\r\nArchetype: Student \r\nCharacter Description:
    \r\n\tMackenzie Joyce is a waitress at a Chinese buffet. Mackenzie wears the
    same thing everyday, practically: jeans and her work shirt. Her legs peaking
    out the bottom of the overworn denim. She would often put her strawberry blonde
    hair up before going in to work. Her work days include refilling the drinks of
    the elderly crowd that rolls in for dinner everyday at four. She takes classes
    at the local community college on the weekends, expecting to graduate with a
    degree in business at the end of the year. \r\nShe still lives with her parents,
    a fact she doesn\u2019t mention whenever making small talk. She justifies it
    by saying she is helping take care of her little sister, Mia. If you ask Mia,
    the best thing about having her sister at home is that she leaves weed in the
    top drawer of the dresser and never notices when the supply goes down. Mackenzie
    pays her share in rent and utilities. Part of her knows that she would be able
    to live on her own, but another part wonders if she could. For the time being,
    her bills are paid by the retirement funds of the golden girls that need drink
    refills every Friday \u201Cnight.\u201D \r\n\tThe calls started on her opening
    shift while she was sweeping the main room. She pulled her phone out of her apron.
    The call showed no name, not even a \u201Cno caller ID\u201D or an \u201Cunknown,\u201Djust
    a blank space where the number should be. She declined the call. The rest of
    the day went as planned. The regulars came in at their regular time. There was
    rarely any new customers. If there was, they had wandered in after driving through
    town and giving up on finding something better. Two hours before closing a new
    customer came in and sat down. \r\n\tHe was thin and tall. He had to almost shrink
    himself down to fit in the chair. He looked old, seventies maybe. His eyes were
    dark and his hair was sparse and gray. His forgettable face had eyes that stared
    miles past anything in the restaurant. He wore a thick coat and dark jeans that
    ran down into black boots. He stared at the table. Mackenzie felt hesitant about
    going over to him. Most people don\u2019t even understand the point of a waitress
    at a buffet, and this man was anything but inviting. \r\nAs time passed, she
    decided she had to greet him. She walked over to his table and set down a glass
    of water. \u201CWelcome in! What can I get you to drink?\u201D He looked up just
    enough so that she knew he heard him. He waved his hand and pointed to the water.
    One could nearly see the bones in his fingers through his thin skin. Mackenzie
    paused for a moment. \u201CWell, let me know if you need anything.\u201D\r\n\tHe
    spent the next hour sipping water, never getting up to get food. If her manager
    was there, he would have probably told her to kick the man out, but it was slow
    tonight. Soon, though, it became time to close and Mackenzie walked over one
    more time. \u201CSorry, sir. We are closing now. Is there anything I can get
    you on your way out?\u201D The man shook his head and began to stand up. He turned
    toward her, seeing her for the first time. \r\n\t\u201CAnswer your phone.\u201D\r\n\tSince
    that day, she has been getting calls more frequently. The first few days she
    got maybe one a day, then they grew. Nearly a week after the man wandered in,
    she was getting nearly seven calls a day. She had reached out to her phone company
    to see if she could block the number, but they had no record of the calls. She
    thought about going to the police but had no way to prove it was happening. Finally,
    Mackenzie decided that she could work without a phone and left it on her bedside
    table.\r\n\tThat day as she opened the door to work, the phone to the restaurant
    rang, an old desk phone with a blue screen. She stared at it as it droned on,
    seemingly forever.  She watched the phone light up and her gaze sank into the
    blue void on the phone\u2019s monitor, not listing any name, number, or location.
    It rang until  her manager came over and picked it up. He nodded his head to
    the voice on the other end of the line. He handed the phone to Mackenzie saying,
    \u201CIt\u2019s for you.\u201D \r\nShe wanted to run away. She wanted to tell
    him to hang up the phone and rip it  from the wall, but she couldn\u2019t. Her
    arm, seemingly against her control, reached for the phone. Shaking, she brought
    it to her ear. The last thing she remembered was hearing the words:\r\n"
